---
import LandingLayout from '../layouts/LandingLayout.astro';
import ProductBlock from '../components/ProductBlock.astro';
import RelatedLinks from '../components/RelatedLinks.astro';
import { page } from '../data/site';

const related = [
  page('/home-maintenance-tracker/'),
  page('/home-maintenance-reminders/'),
  page('/digital-home-binder/'),
  page('/appliance-maintenance-records/'),
  page('/home-repair-cost-tracker/'),
  page('/home-contractor-records/'),
  page('/resources/home-maintenance-records-to-keep/')
];
---
<LandingLayout
  title="Home Maintenance Records and Repair History App | Around the House"
  description="Keep home maintenance records and repair history by appliance or system. Record dates, work performed, service providers, costs, photos, receipts, and what needs to happen next."
  canonicalPath="/home-maintenance-records/"
  breadcrumbLabel="Home maintenance records"
  h1="Home maintenance records that become more useful over time"
  intro="When something breaks, the useful questions are usually historical: When was it last serviced? What was replaced? Who did the work? What did it cost? Around The House keeps those answers with the part of the home they describe."
  ctaHeading="Write down the last repair you remember."
  ctaBody="Add its date, provider, cost, and one useful note. That is enough to begin a dependable history."
>
  <section>
    <h2>What to record after maintenance or a repair</h2>
    <p>
      A good maintenance log does not need to become a diary. Capture the facts that will change a future
      decision, make the next service call easier, or explain the home's condition to someone else.
    </p>
    <ul class="qa-list">
      <li><strong>Date</strong><span>When the work was completed, not merely when it was scheduled.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Type of work</strong><span>Repair, replacement, cleaning, inspection, or preventive maintenance.</span></li>
      <li><strong>What was done</strong><span>A short description of the work and any parts that were replaced.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Service provider</strong><span>The person or company you may need to contact again.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Cost</strong><span>The amount paid, including enough context to compare future work.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Evidence</strong><span>Photos, receipts, invoices, or notes that may matter for a warranty or resale.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <ProductBlock
    device="iphone"
    src="/assets/screenshots/new/iphone-item-history.webp"
    alt="Maintenance history for a home item in Around The House on iPhone, with completed work, dates, providers, and costs"
  >
    <h2>Keep one running history for each item</h2>
    <p>
      A furnace should have its own history. So should a roof, water heater, dishwasher, deck, or other part of
      the home worth maintaining. Each completed entry appears in date order on that item's record.
    </p>
    <p>
      This makes it possible to see patterns: a repair that keeps returning, an appliance that is becoming more
      expensive, or a system that has not received preventive service in several years.
    </p>
  </ProductBlock>

  <ProductBlock
    device="ipad"
    flip
    src="/assets/screenshots/new/ipad-item-record.webp"
    alt="A complete home item record in Around The House on iPad with photos, identifying details, maintenance history, reminders, and documents"
  >
    <h2>The history sits beside the identifying details</h2>
    <p>
      Repair notes are easier to use when the model number, serial number, installation date, photos, manual,
      and warranty are nearby. Around The House keeps those details on one item record instead of splitting them
      among a calendar, spreadsheet, photo library, and document folder.
    </p>
    <p>
      When a technician asks what happened last time, the answer does not depend on remembering which app holds it.
    </p>
  </ProductBlock>

  <section>
    <h2>See costs without losing the story behind them</h2>
    <p>
      Each maintenance entry can include a cost, allowing reports to summarize spending over time. The total is
      useful, but the underlying history remains available: which item required the expense, what kind of work it
      was, and who performed it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Records make the next reminder smarter</h2>
    <p>
      Maintenance history answers what happened. A recurring reminder carries that knowledge forward by putting
      the next filter change, inspection, or service visit back on the schedule. Together they form a continuous
      record instead of a collection of disconnected alerts.
    </p>
  </section>

  <RelatedLinks heading="Continue organizing maintenance" items={related} />
</LandingLayout>
